{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Into their council let not my soul come.</b> Rav Aharon said that the meaning of אל תבוא נפשי is like 'ובא השמש' (Ecclesiastes 1:5), and the sense is that I would not want to be outside their counsel. But his interpretation is the opposite of the matter, for what reason is there for the implements of [?], and did he not say to both of them, 'עכרתם אותי' (Genesis 34:30)? And in truth Jacob and his household were endangered because of them — were it not for the terror of Elohim, they would have brought about the destruction of everything. So too he interpreted 'ארור אפם' and 'וברצונם עקרו שור' as 'ארור כי אפם עז' — and these interpretations are cold. בסודם אל תבא נפשי: R. Moshe haKohen z\"l said that כבודי is like נפשי, and there are many in the book of Psalms like it. And he interpreted well, for the meaning is doubled in the manner of prophecies — 'שאל אביך' (Deuteronomy 32:7), 'מה אקוב' (Numbers 23:8). And so בסודם is like בקהלם, and תבא is like תחד, and נפשי is like כבודי. And Rabbi Yehuda ben Bil'am the Spaniard said that R. Moshe erred, for the glory of the soul is the body, like 'עדי בצואר' in the sense of 'המשביע בטור עדיך' (Psalms 103:5), and his proof is 'וכבודי לעפר ישכן סלה' (Psalms 7:6). But I say that the chief [lit. 'prince'] of Yehuda erred, for Scripture says 'למען יזמרך כבוד' (Psalms 30:13), 'לכן שמח לבי ויגל כבודי' (ibid. 16:9) — alongside 'בשרי' which is the body, and 'וכבודי לעפר ישכן' is by way of metaphor, that one humbles one's soul even to the dust, than which there is nothing lower. And I will give him a trustworthy witness, and it is 'דבקה לעפר נפשי' (Psalms 119:25)." "<b>תחד.</b> From the root יחד. And according to the opinion of R. Moshe haKohen the grammarian z\"l, he left the hidden [alef] in place of one alef, and so he said regarding 'לא תֵשָם' (Genesis 47:19)." "And the meaning of בסודם is like 'בהוסדם יחד עלי' (Psalms 31:14). After their devices are violence, let not my soul come into their council." "הרגו איש. A noun of species, like 'שור וחמור' (Genesis 32:6). And the hint is to the men of the city of Shechem." "<b>שור.</b> A wall, and so 'צעדה עלי שור' (Genesis 49:22). And I have already explained in the book of Moznayim that the holem and the shurek interchange with each other." "'ועקרו' — like 'סוסיהם תעקר' (Joshua 11:6). And this is a sign that the city of Shechem was great, for it was surrounded by a wall."], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {0 "The phrase 'כלי המס' in the critique of Rav Aharon is textually difficult; it may be corrupt. Read hyper-literally as 'implements of [?].' Also, the phrase 'שר יהודה' (chief/prince of Judah) for R. Yehuda ben Bil'am is an epithet resolved by inference; it may be an honorific or possibly a textual variant.", 1 "The phrase 'הניח הנעלם תחת אלף אחד' is grammatically opaque; translated hyper-literally. The reference is to R. Moshe haKohen's view that a quiescent or implied alef underlies certain spelling anomalies."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:48:33.318255790Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}